=== plain-80: width 80, height 24, profile notty, dark, glyphs ascii, plain
# play backup/default ------------------------------------------$
$
# Note$
# Play mode: hardn's own screens and prompts, on the real$
# plan/apply engine against a simulated host, hostfake's$
# recording host.System, whose commands a model of the host$
# answers. Nothing on this machine changes: every command and$
# file is the fake's, and its record is shown when play ends.$
$
# Info: backup/default$
# web-01.example.net runs Debian 13. hardn.yml keeps the$
# defaults: backups on, in /var/backups/hardn, not made yet.$
$
# Backup Settings ##############################################$
$
# Backup Configuration -----------------------------------------$
$
  [+] Backup State         Enabled$
  [+] Backup Path          /var/backups/hardn Not created yet$
$
  Select an option$
$
   1) Backup State         Turn backups off$
   2) Backup Path          Change backup path$
   3) Verify Directory     Check the path, writing nothing$
   4) Backup Runs          None yet$
$
   0) Return$
$
  > Enter selection [0-4 or q]: 2$
$
# Change Backup Path ###########################################$
$
  Enter new backup path [0 or q]: backups$
  (x) Not an absolute path$
  Enter new backup path [0 or q]: /var/backups//hardn/$
  (x) Write it as /var/backups/hardn$
  Enter new backup path [0 or q]: /tmp/hardn$
  (x) Others can change /tmp$
  Enter new backup path [0 or q]: /etc/os-release$
  (x) Not a directory$
  Enter new backup path [0 or q]: /var/backups/hardn$
  (x) /var/backups/hardn is the backup path already$
  Enter new backup path [0 or q]: /srv/backups/hardn$
$
# Backup Configuration -----------------------------------------$
$
  [+] Backup Path          /srv/backups/hardn Not created yet$
$
   -  Save the configuration$
      /etc/hardn/hardn.yml$
      @@ -1,4 +1,4 @@$
       # hardn's configuration (web-01.example.net)$
       username: alice$
       enableBackups: true               # Backup files before$
      | modifying them$
      -backupPath: "/var/backups/hardn"  # Path to store backups$
      +backupPath: "/srv/backups/hardn"  # Path to store backups$
$
  Use /srv/backups/hardn? [y/N, 0 or q]: y$
$
  [+] Save the configuration  Done$
$
# Operation Successful$
$
  Backup of /etc/hardn/hardn.yml kept in$
  /srv/backups/hardn/20261020T120000Z-xxxxxxxx$
$
# Backup Settings ##############################################$
$
# Backup Configuration -----------------------------------------$
$
  [+] Backup State         Enabled$
  [+] Backup Path          /srv/backups/hardn Root only$
$
  Select an option$
$
   1) Backup State         Turn backups off$
   2) Backup Path          Change backup path$
   3) Verify Directory     Check the path, writing nothing$
   4) Backup Runs          1 run$
$
   0) Return$
$
  > Enter selection [0-4 or q]: 0$
$
# simulated system ---------------------------------------------$
$
# Info: backup/default$
# Recorded by the fake, not done: what each step ran on web-$
# 01.example.net, and the files it changed, in order; hardn's$
# own journal, run directory and backups left out.$
$
   -  Applied: Save the configuration$
      /etc/hardn/hardn.yml$
$

